  "strategy": "We are given an initial_h_values (parent solution with 512 samples). We need to find a construction that achieves C5 <= 0.3808.\n\nApproach and rationale:\nThe current best solution has C5=0.380870. We need to minimize the maximum correlation between h(x) and (1-h(x+k)) over all shifts k.\n\nThe previous implementation uses:\n- Multi-resolution optimization (starting from several candidate sizes and up-sampling to 512)\n- Local search using guided hill climbing and SLSQP with softmax surrogate\n- Gradient-based refinement\n\nOur approach to improve:\n1. Use 4 parent solutions (at n=256) to start the search\n2. Scale the parent solutions to 512 points\n3. Use aggressive directional perturbation based on the gradient of the worst-shift\n4. Optimize each perturbed candidate with unlimited iterations but time-constrained\n5. Use heavy regularization to prevent overfitting to the worst-shift\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe seek to minimize C5(max) subject to:\n  (1) 0 <= h_i <= 1 for all i\n  (2) \u2211h_i = n/2\n\nThe function to minimize is the worst shift overlap:\n  f(h) = max_k (np.dot(h, np.roll(1-h, k)) * dx) for k in [-n+1, n-1]\n\nIn our discrete version with step size dx=2/512:\n  C5 = max_k corr(h, 1-h)[k] * (2/512)\n\nImplementation procedure:\nWe will:\n1. Validate the provided initial_h_values and a few fallback positions\n2. Use 4 parent solutions at resolution 256 and scale them to 512\n3. For each parent, generate 4 children by directional perturbation\n   - Compute worst-shift gradient: g = worst_gradient(h)\n   - Move away from positive-gradient regions and toward negative-gradient regions\n4. For each child, perform heavy optimization:\n   - Guided hill climbing with many iterations\n   - Multiple SLSQP runs with increasing beta\n   - Gradient refinement\n   - Final hill climbing\n5. Keep the best improving solution\n\nSearch choices:\n1. Resolution: fixed at 512\n2. Parent solutions: 4 variations from the given parent\n3. Perturbation strategy:\n   - Follow the worst-shift gradient for larger steps\n   - Apply directional perturbation: transfer from highest positive gradient index to lowest negative gradient index\n4. Optimization parameters:\n   - Guided hill climb: max_iters=4000, step_scale=0.8 (initial)\n   - SLSQP: start_beta=400, then 2*start_beta\n   - Gradient refinement: max_iter=10\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Time budget: 1100 seconds\n- Dominant cost: 4 parents \u00d7 4 children \u00d7 optimization (each optimization takes ~20 seconds worst-case)\n- Check elapsed time after each optimization\n- Use parent fallback if optimization fails\n\nValidation:\n- Ensure sum(h)=n/2 exactly after each transformation\n- Clip and enforce bounds with enforce_sum function\n- Return the best solution found before time expires\n\nImplementation details:\n1. Validate inputs and set up random seed\n2. Precompute the four parent candidates by directional perturbation from the parent\n3. Scale parents from 256 to 512 using linear interpolation, then enforce sum\n4. For each parent candidate:\n   - Perturb to create child candidates\n   - Perform local optimization with scheduled hill climbs and SLSQP\n   - Track best solution\n5. Return best solution found\n\nHelper functions needed:\n- worst_gradient to compute gradient for the worst-shift overlap\n- guided_perturb to direct perturbations based on gradient\n- guided_hill_climb to perform gradient-based hill climbing\n- slsqp_optimize to use SLSQP with softmax surrogate\n- gradient_refine to perform gradient-based polishing\n- enforce_sum to maintain integral constraint",
